**On the vision:** nothing ambiguous blocked this ruling, but one observation for the owner: the durable-evidence section is doing double duty — it both *mandates* measurements and, read closely, *sequences* them ("the thing that notices is part of the decision"). BL-5 existed because that sentence doesn't say what to do when the decision's observable surface spans multiple future plans. One added sentence — e.g. "a measurement ships with the change that makes it observable" — would have decided BL-5 directly and will decide its siblings (S3, S5) without another oracle round; OD-4 records that reading, but it currently rests on my inference rather than the owner's words.
